452
00:33:15,756 --> 00:33:15,956
Great.

453
00:33:18,338 --> 00:33:19,319
All right.

454
00:33:19,539 --> 00:33:26,885
Next one is that you do not have local historic district design guidelines and

455
00:33:27,453 --> 00:33:32,697
You're not unlike lots of other historic district commissions in Massachusetts that don't have design guidelines either.

456
00:33:33,918 --> 00:33:37,161
This is a really important thing for a historic district commission to have.

457
00:33:37,661 --> 00:33:43,706
A good set of design guidelines are great for you as a historic district commission, and they're great for the applicants.

458
00:33:44,887 --> 00:33:56,057
They're great for you so that when you are taking a vote, you can actually refer to the design guidelines and say, oh, OK, this project that

459
00:33:57,580 --> 00:34:01,062
that's being applied for right here, this meets our design guidelines.

460
00:34:01,522 --> 00:34:18,451
And you can actually, instead of having a motion that has to be complicated, you can actually have a motion that says, I vote to make a motion to approve this project because it meets section 3.2 of the design guidelines.

461
00:34:18,511 --> 00:34:22,073
It just makes it a lot easier for you to be able to have these design guidelines to refer to.

462
00:34:23,773 --> 00:34:43,550
Also is great for applicants, property owners, developers, whoever, contractors, because instead of wondering how you as a district commissioner are gonna make your decisions, they can actually download the design guidelines and quickly look through to the right page.

463
00:34:44,230 --> 00:34:46,292
And let's say it was chimneys.

464
00:34:46,732 --> 00:34:49,554
You're gonna flip through the design guidelines and flip to the page on chimneys.

465
00:34:49,955 --> 00:34:51,836
And it's gonna talk about all the kinds of things

466
00:34:53,287 --> 00:35:00,992
related to chimneys in terms of appropriate work and incompatible work as well.

467
00:35:03,674 --> 00:35:10,398
So it makes it really easy for everyone to understand the kinds of things that you're going to approve as a district commission and the kinds of things that you're not going to approve.

468
00:35:11,439 --> 00:35:16,142
And this is a really helpful way to think about new local historic districts as well.

469
00:35:18,042 --> 00:35:23,685
because it really puts it on paper how your process works, how your decision-making process works.

470
00:35:24,405 --> 00:35:28,727
So if anyone's wondering, you can just refer to them and say, here's the design guidelines.

471
00:35:28,787 --> 00:35:31,868
Here's how we go about our decision-making process.

472
00:35:34,489 --> 00:35:35,870
Really would recommend design guidelines.

473
00:35:36,110 --> 00:35:42,153
Okay, next one to think about is some criteria when you're pursuing new local historic districts.

474
00:35:43,914 --> 00:35:47,075
The idea of actually usually of actually having some

475
00:35:47,559 --> 00:35:58,323
some locally based criteria that sets up what's going to be needed for you to support a local historic district.

476
00:35:58,383 --> 00:36:12,128
So it might be based on its historic associations, architecture, basically its significance, but it's going to have maybe seven, eight, 10 different criteria

477
00:36:12,724 --> 00:36:18,466
for how you would decide whether you would support local historic district in this area.

478
00:36:19,446 --> 00:36:23,288
So lots of commissions use the national register criteria.

479
00:36:24,548 --> 00:36:26,909
I think the downside of that is that that's nationally based.

480
00:36:26,949 --> 00:36:29,330
It's a national criteria.

481
00:36:29,910 --> 00:36:34,551
And I really liked the idea of having some locally based eligibility criteria.

482
00:36:34,571 --> 00:36:37,312
So it really comes from the community itself.

483
00:36:39,153 --> 00:36:42,534
So a good thing to think about just in terms of looking at some other criteria

484
00:36:43,048 --> 00:36:46,530
from cities across the country that have done this already.

485
00:36:49,371 --> 00:36:51,352
Pursuing single building local historic districts.

486
00:36:52,132 --> 00:36:56,374
Another idea to think about in terms of strategies.

487
00:36:57,935 --> 00:36:59,436
And there's two different ways to think about this.

488
00:36:59,856 --> 00:37:11,702
So when the Medford Historical Commission puts a building under demolition delay, you have the ability to pursue a single building local historic district at that time.

489
00:37:14,215 --> 00:37:24,679
The other way to think about local historic districts is that property owners actually proactively come to you because they would like to have a local historic district on their property.

490
00:37:25,099 --> 00:37:29,221
And so the one on the bottom left is an example of that.

491
00:37:30,261 --> 00:37:38,384
And it's a property owner that basically came forward and said, this house that I live in was actually lived in by Sylvia Plath.

492
00:37:45,105 --> 00:37:48,249
And we want to make sure that this building is well protected into the future.

493
00:37:49,690 --> 00:37:53,675
So whether we own it or not, we would like you to establish a local historic district on our house.

494
00:37:54,496 --> 00:38:05,788
And so lots of towns have actually really made this as a strategy to talk to property owners that would like to make sure that their houses are well protected into the future.

495
00:38:11,498 --> 00:38:16,822
The next idea is just to think about establishing local historic districts as part of a strategic plan.

496
00:38:17,683 --> 00:38:36,038
Really getting out there citywide, thinking neighborhood by neighborhood, and thinking about not just where local historic districts make sense based on your local criteria of what would meet a local historic district,

497
00:38:36,614 --> 00:38:48,664
but actually really talking to a lot of the property owners themselves and finding out where those pockets of support, where are the neighborhoods that really say, yes, we're concerned about what might happen to our neighborhood in the future.

498
00:38:49,204 --> 00:38:52,487
And we are interested in hearing more about a local historic district.

499
00:38:53,508 --> 00:38:59,933
So that's really trying to think citywide instead of responding to sort of threats that are coming in.

500
00:39:00,193 --> 00:39:02,495
This is a proactive approach.

501
00:39:03,255 --> 00:39:05,097
So my two photos here, I put them in.

502
00:39:05,608 --> 00:39:07,548
This is how historic preservation often works.

503
00:39:08,609 --> 00:39:14,770
It's campaigns, it's thinking about things in a reactive sense.

504
00:39:15,491 --> 00:39:24,993
And a strategic plan really tries to get you out there and think proactively about how best to pursue local historic districts citywide.

505
00:39:27,574 --> 00:39:29,074
Understanding property owner support.

506
00:39:29,714 --> 00:39:33,015
That is all about recognizing that

507
00:39:35,146 --> 00:39:43,873
The way local historic districts work is that theoretically, no one in a local historic district has to support the local historic district.

508
00:39:44,233 --> 00:39:46,795
It is entirely a function of your city council.

509
00:39:48,777 --> 00:39:55,703
However, always have to remember that local historic districts are part of the local political process.

510
00:39:56,463 --> 00:40:00,987
And so really, really recognizing that

511
00:40:02,655 --> 00:40:10,138
Local historic district creation is going to work best when it comes from the neighborhood themselves.

512
00:40:12,199 --> 00:40:27,125
So I put up a sign here on the upper left, that's from the town of Reading and that neighborhood that was pursuing local historic district, very, very grassroots, all the neighbors absolutely wanted the local historic district and it passed that way.

513
00:40:32,343 --> 00:40:44,653
So it doesn't have to be, again, it doesn't have to be all owner support, but really trying to think about not the district commission taking the lead.

514
00:40:44,673 --> 00:40:51,799
I think that's the key point here is that you as the district commission isn't necessarily the lead in pursuing this.

515
00:40:51,859 --> 00:40:59,846
It really works best when the neighbors themselves are the ones that are not only filling out the survey that says they want a local historic district,

516
00:41:00,187 --> 00:41:08,614
but it's the neighbors themselves that are showing up at your public hearing, at the city council public hearing, and vocally saying, we want a local historic district.

517
00:41:12,737 --> 00:41:14,779
There's another technique that's in use in Cambridge.

518
00:41:17,441 --> 00:41:20,263
It's sort of the lighter version of a local historic district.

519
00:41:21,264 --> 00:41:27,769
It means that there's a lot more things that are exempt from review or only have an advisory level review.

520
00:41:28,975 --> 00:41:32,137
So there are other options besides just local historic districts.

521
00:41:32,177 --> 00:41:34,218
So this is something to think about.

522
00:41:34,458 --> 00:41:39,661
And you have a wonderful example that's been around for decades in Cambridge, right next door.

523
00:41:44,164 --> 00:41:49,046
And then cooperating with the Medford Historical Commission, really important too.

524
00:41:50,027 --> 00:41:54,269
Both commissions are really key to protecting historic buildings to local historic districts.

525
00:41:55,490 --> 00:41:58,552
And I mentioned that for a couple of reasons.

526
00:42:02,520 --> 00:42:20,715
One through all of the documenting historic resources citywide, the local historical commission is gonna have a really good sense of where based on the architectural significance of the area, where it would make sense to have some local historic districts.

527
00:42:23,198 --> 00:42:25,880
The other reason that local historical commissions really help

528
00:42:29,188 --> 00:42:53,624
in establishing local historic districts are because when the local historical commission does things like community outreach, education, just developing programs that really support the interest of historic resources citywide, that in general can be one of those things that can really

529
00:42:57,972 --> 00:43:05,478
help to, on a citywide level, help everyone to recognize that they live in a really special place.

530
00:43:06,419 --> 00:43:16,707
And that's going to mean, in some cases, that there's going to be more interest in how we protect the areas that we live through things like a local historic district.

531
00:43:21,342 --> 00:43:27,409
Annual work program is a great idea to just to get you in your work in the public eye a little bit more.

532
00:43:27,429 --> 00:43:30,232
I think it's great to put an annual work program together.

533
00:43:30,772 --> 00:43:35,317
And then once a year, ask the city council if you can present your annual work program in front of them.

534
00:43:37,019 --> 00:43:41,183
And so that really helps you get your message out about all the things that you've accomplished.

535
00:43:42,345 --> 00:43:51,650
an annual work program can make you feel really good, too, because it's a great sense of accomplishment when you look back at the end of the year and say, yeah, these are the things that we were able to accomplish.

536
00:43:51,670 --> 00:43:57,634
All right, I'm going to move on to our next one here.

