595
00:48:34,194 --> 00:48:45,581
All right, so administering local historic districts, the report goes over all the different kinds of ways that you as a district commission have to administer your local historic district.

596
00:48:46,121 --> 00:48:51,444
So that's application process in terms of taking in applications, making sure they're complete, things like that.

597
00:48:51,844 --> 00:49:01,170
The hearing process, the decision-making process, the certificates, issuing certificates, filing all the paperwork with the city clerk,

598
00:49:02,237 --> 00:49:04,238
and then monitoring projects after that.

599
00:49:04,338 --> 00:49:10,843
So there's a long list of things that need to be done to administer any local historic district.

600
00:49:13,464 --> 00:49:22,530
And in terms of staff assistance, there's some real benefits to having somebody help out in terms of that administration.

601
00:49:23,271 --> 00:49:31,456
One of the big ones that just make the open meeting law, just having somebody in city hall that can make sure all the requirements of the open meeting law are being followed,

602
00:49:31,877 --> 00:49:34,439
that all the proper procedures are being followed under state law.

603
00:49:35,379 --> 00:49:48,988
And yeah, it can help you avoid appeals sometimes because if there's some procedure that hasn't been followed, that's gonna be subject to litigation right there.

604
00:49:51,189 --> 00:49:59,274
You are a certified local government and the staff assistance can really help in terms of the annual report that gets submitted for

605
00:50:01,371 --> 00:50:01,971
being a CLG.

606
00:50:01,991 --> 00:50:25,176
And then I think just the volunteer time commitments, when you're really spending a lot of time as volunteers doing some of the routine administration of a local historic district, that really is a burden on city volunteers and can impact the ability to maintain a community,

607
00:50:27,837 --> 00:50:36,620
volunteers on any kind of board and commission just because they've got to spend all that extra time doing the administration instead of just the meetings and the hearings themselves.

608
00:50:38,241 --> 00:50:42,682
There's basically three types of staff assistance that's provided to historic district commissions.

609
00:50:43,242 --> 00:50:45,723
There's professional preservation planner staff support.

610
00:50:45,903 --> 00:50:49,484
So by that, I mean, somebody that actually has a degree in historic preservation.

611
00:50:51,165 --> 00:50:56,827
And so local cities that have a professional preservation planner on staff,

612
00:50:57,763 --> 00:51:09,805
So that's going to be Cambridge, Boston, Newton, Brookline, Salem, Lowell are all going to have professional preservation planners that have a degree in preservation.

613
00:51:10,425 --> 00:51:24,007
The next one down is going to be general planner, where the planning director, the assistant planner, somebody with a planning degree, but not necessarily a background in historic preservation offers the staff support.

614
00:51:25,308 --> 00:51:26,188
That can be pretty good.

615
00:51:27,689 --> 00:51:34,872
because at least they're gonna be familiar with state law and open meeting law and the basics of city government.

616
00:51:36,633 --> 00:51:39,634
Then the routine administrative staff support is the third one.

617
00:51:40,134 --> 00:51:56,521
And that's really just gonna be the minimum level of staff support in terms of making sure that things are posted properly and minutes are taken, just really more of the routine kinds of things.

618
00:52:00,481 --> 00:52:07,505
So the report goes through a list of all the communities that we were able to contact and hear the kinds of things that they have.

619
00:52:08,326 --> 00:52:14,930
